    "body": "The open pit at the former Jeffrey asbestos mine in Quebec. Credit: Credit: Chilanga Cement/Wikimedia Commons\nPosted By: Blair McBride October 16, 2026\nFew chapters in Quebec’s mining history are as dramatic as that of asbestos, first discovered near Thetford in the province’s southeast in 1876. Three years later, another major find about 60 km away produced the Jeffrey mine. The town of Asbestos (now Val-des-Sources) grew around it.\nUsed as a cheap source of insulation and fireproofing, commercial asbestos mining expanded into the 20th century in clusters around Thetford and Asbestos. More than a dozen operations – mostly open-pit – grew by the 1930s.\nWorld leader\nIn 1930, The Northern Miner reported that Quebec mines had extracted 5.1 million tons of asbestos-bearing rock in 1929. The previous year, shipments of processed asbestos totalled $11.2 million in value – about $203 million today – several times the amount of any competing country.\n“Canada supplies about 70% of the world’s asbestos from a serpentine belt running through the Eastern townships of the province of Quebec to the Vermont border,” The Miner reported in 1943.\nBut medical knowledge was less advanced in the early 20th century, and asbestos soon faced a reckoning.\nAlarming medical report\nMost long-term workers at Quebec asbestos mines suffered from lung problems, including cancer, from breathing in asbestos fibres, a 1975 report from Mount Sinai Hospital in New York stated. That sparked a strike in Thetford, where thousands of employees walked off the job demanding better working conditions.\nIn the following decades, Quebec’s asbestos industry declined as growing evidence of its health risks prompted stricter regulations and curtailed demand.\nIt all came to an end in 2010 when the Jeffrey mine closed. Almost all of its last 150,000 tonnes of asbestos – worth $90 million – went to developing countries.\nThe Quebec government made an awkward, last-ditch attempt to reopen Jeffrey in 2012 as an underground operation, even approving a $58-million loan for it. But the newly elected Parti Québécois government withdrew the financing, finally closing Quebec’s asbestos chapter.\nThis is part of The Northern Miner ‘s From The Archives series, where we link current developments, like rising precious metals prices incenting further exploration and mining, with news items from our 111 years of archives. The Miner is one of Canada’s oldest mining publications.\nPrint\nTAG: Archives\nREGION: Canada\nRepublish this article",
